        <section class="reference-section">
          <h2>DNS Record Type Reference</h2>

          <table class="reference-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Purpose</th>
                <th>Example</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><code>A</code></td>
                <td>Maps a hostname to an IPv4 address.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>www 300 IN A 192.0.2.10</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>AAAA</code></td>
                <td>Maps a hostname to an IPv6 address.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>www 300 IN AAAA 2001:db8::10</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>CNAME</code></td>
                <td>Creates an alias for another hostname.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>www 300 IN CNAME example.com.</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>MX</code></td>
                <td>Specifies a mail server and priority.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>@ 300 IN MX 10 mail.example.com.</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>TXT</code></td>
                <td>Stores SPF, DKIM, DMARC or verification text.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>@ 300 IN TXT "v=spf1 -all"</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>NS</code></td>
                <td>Defines an authoritative name server.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>@ 300 IN NS ns1.example.com.</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>PTR</code></td>
                <td>Maps a reverse DNS name to a hostname.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>10 300 IN PTR server.example.com.</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>SRV</code></td>
                <td>Defines a service target and port.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>
                    _sip._tcp 300 IN SRV 10 5 5060 sip.example.com.
                  </code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>CAA</code></td>
                <td>Restricts certificate-authority issuance.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>@ 300 IN CAA 0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code>
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <td><code>SOA</code></td>
                <td>Defines zone authority and timing values.</td>
                <td>
                  <code>@ 300 IN SOA ns1.example.com. hostmaster.example.com. (...)</code>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <section class="reference-section">
          <h2>DNS Record Configuration Tips</h2>

          <div class="info-grid">
            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>Use Correct TTL</strong>
              <p>
                Use a lower TTL before planned DNS changes and increase it
                after the configuration becomes stable.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>Use Complete Targets</strong>
              <p>
                CNAME, MX, NS, PTR, SRV and SOA hostname targets should normally
                be complete domain names.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>MX Priority</strong>
              <p>
                A lower MX priority number is preferred before a higher
                priority number.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>CNAME Restriction</strong>
              <p>
                A CNAME name should generally not have other record types at
                the same owner name.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>TXT Quoting</strong>
              <p>
                TXT values are represented inside quotation marks in standard
                DNS zone-file syntax.
              </p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card">
              <strong>DNS Propagation</strong>
              <p>
                Cached records can remain visible until their previous TTL
                expires.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>
      </section>
    </main>

